<http://model.geneontology.org/6ab067da00013223> rdf:type owl:Ontology ;
                                                  owl:versionIRI <http://model.geneontology.org/6ab067da00013223> ;
                                                  <http://geneontology.org/lego/conforms_to_gpad> "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
                                                  <http://geneontology.org/lego/modelstate> "development" ;
                                                  <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/contributor> "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2979-8613"^^xsd:string ;
                                                  <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/date> "2026-11-16"^^xsd:string ;
                                                  <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/title> "Fatty acid beta-oxidation via glucagon-AMPK-Acacb (Mouse)"^^xsd:string ;
                                                  <http://purl.org/pav/providedBy> "http://geneontology.org"^^xsd:string ,
                                                                                   "https://www.uniprot.org" ;
                                                  rdfs:comment "Curated with GO AI HUB. Mouse hepatic fatty acid beta-oxidation driven by glucagon: Gcg-Gcgr-Gnas-Adcy6-cAMP-PKA signaling and AMPK inhibition of Acacb, relieving malonyl-CoA inhibition of Cpt1a."^^xsd:string ,
                                                               "Focused model of glucagon-driven hepatic lipid oxidation through relief of malonyl-CoA inhibition of Cpt1a. AMPK enabled by GO:0031588 because no paper resolves the catalytic isoform. Enabled-by evidence (2026-11-16): IMP PMID:21459323 (Li 2011: AMPK activators no longer induce SREBP-1c Ser372 phosphorylation in AMPKalpha1/alpha2 double-knockout mouse embryonic fibroblasts, Fig 7C; dominant-negative AMPK reduces it in primary mouse hepatocytes, Fig 7G; purified rat AMPK phosphorylates SREBP-1c Ser372 in an in vitro [32P]-ATP kinase assay, Fig 7A). Replaces IDA PMID:19046568, whose Fig 2A shows Thr172 phosphorylation only. Contrary evidence: PMID:32184359 found no effect of glucagon on AMPK activity in mouse hepatocytes. The glucagon-specific ACC data are from rat hepatocytes and do not resolve ACC1 from ACC2 (PMID:2898386 AMPK not PKA; PMID:711753, PMID:632284 malonyl-CoA). In mouse liver, Acc1/Acc2 are redundant: single Acc2-S212A knock-in had minimal effects (PMID:24185692); liver malonyl-CoA was unchanged in Acc2-/- (PMID:11283375); see also PMID:20368432. Retracted, not used: PMID:20663988."^^xsd:string ,
                                                               "Gcg, Gcgr and Gnas units (updated 2026-11-10): ISS (GO_REF:0000024) from human GCG (UniProtKB:P01275) / human GCGR (UniProtKB:P47871) on Gcg 'enabled by', 'has input Gcgr', 'part of adenylate cyclase-activating G protein-coupled receptor signaling pathway' and on Gcgr 'enabled by', 'has input Gnas', 'part of' the same pathway and 'directly positively regulates Gnas' are based on Qiao 2020 (PMID:32193322; cryo-EM structures of human GCGR bound to glucagon with Gs or Gi1, supported by mutagenesis and functional assays). Gnas 'part of adenylate cyclase-activating G protein-coupled receptor signaling pathway' also has ISS from human GNAS (UniProtKB:P63092) based on Qiao 2020 (glucagon-induced Gs activation by NanoBiT, Fig 4I,K; glucagon-induced cAMP accumulation, Fig 4E,G). Chen 2005 (PMID:16239968) is not used on this fact: it measures blood glucose after glucagon (Fig 3A), not adenylyl cyclase activity or cAMP. Gcgr -> Gnas also keeps IMP PMID:16239968 (Chen 2005, liver-specific Gsalpha knockout mice lose the hyperglycemic response to glucagon, Fig 3A). Longuet 2008 (PMID:19046568) uses exogenous glucagon only and does not show Gcg hormone activity or glucagon acting on Gcgr."^^xsd:string ,
                                                               "Input to AMPK (GO:0031588) (updated 2026-11-16). Glucagon reaches AMPK through Gcgr -> Gnas -> Adcy6 -> AMPK. Adcy6 (adenylate cyclase activity) -> AMPK uses 'causally upstream of, positive effect' (RO:0002304) because the intervening steps are not modelled. Evidence: IC with GO:0070730 (cAMP transport), PMID:28515141 (Lv 2017, mouse primary hepatocytes): Ad-AC6 raises extracellular cAMP (Fig 2D-E); cAMP export via MRP4 is needed (probenecid, MRP4 RNAi, Fig 2F-I, 3C); extracellular cAMP raises AMPK phosphorylation (Fig 3A); 24 h glucagon raises AMPK phosphorylation (Fig 3B), reduced by AMPCP (5'-nucleotidase inhibitor) and NBTI (CNT2 adenosine transporter inhibitor) but not DPSPX (adenosine receptor antagonist) (Fig 3D; legend says cAMP-induced). Proposed route: exported cAMP -> AMP -> adenosine -> CNT2 uptake -> AMPK; PKA-independent but cAMP-dependent. IC because Lv never measures AMPK after manipulating AC6; readout is phosphorylation, not kinase activity. Glucagon increases AMPKalpha Thr172 phosphorylation in mouse primary hepatocytes (PMID:19046568, Longuet 2008, Fig 2A; immunoblot, no kinase assay), but the route is not resolved; a direct Gcgr -> AMPK edge carrying this IDA was removed on 2026-11-16 as redundant with the path above. Not modelled: lower hepatic energy state via gluconeogenesis/PEPCK-C (PMID:19662685, no mouse loss-of-function test); Ca2+/CaMKK2 not verified in liver. A PKA -> AMPK edge was removed on 2026-11-09: there is no loss-of-function evidence that PKA is needed for glucagon to activate hepatic AMPK; the PKA inhibitor H89 did not prevent glucagon's AMPK-dependent effects (PMID:19046568, Fig 2C, 3B-C); Kimball 2004 (PMID:15494402, perfused rat liver) proposes PKA -> LKB1 -> AMPK by association only; and PKA phosphorylates AMPKalpha1 at Ser173 and inhibits its activation by LKB1 in mouse adipocytes (PMID:19942859). Limits: PKA independence rests on a single non-selective inhibitor (H89). Contrary evidence: glucagon did not change AMPK activity in mouse hepatocytes in PMID:32184359."^^xsd:string ,
                                                               "Palmitoylcarnitine start location for Slc25a20 (added 2026-11-16) = mitochondrial intermembrane space (GO:0005758), IC with GO:0005476, PMID:42223129, same pattern as the matrix end location. CPT1 active site faces the cytosol (Fraser 1997, PMID:9169604, rat liver); passage across the outer membrane (VDAC) is not modelled."^^xsd:string ,
                                                               "Transcriptional arms (updated 2026-11-10). Creb1: PKA has input Creb1 and PKA -> Creb1 (directly positively regulates) = IDA PMID:23292513 (Miller 2013, Fig 2A-B: glucagon and the PKA agonist Sp-8-Br-cAMPS-AM induce CREB phosphorylation in mouse primary hepatocytes; PKA isoform not resolved, phospho-site not stated, no in-vitro kinase assay and no CREB transcriptional readout - activation of CREB by phosphorylation rests on Gonzalez & Montminy 1989, PMID:2573431, CREB Ser-133 in rat PC12 cells). Rat ISS (RGD:3389) removed from both edges on 2026-11-10. Louet 2002 (PMID:12107181, Fig 6) shows that PKA-Calpha and CREB both act on the L-CPT I promoter but not that PKA acts on CREB; Creb1 has input Cpt1a gene via the L-CPT I CRE and indirectly positively regulates Cpt1a (ISS rat Creb1 RGD:620218, PMID:12107181 Fig 6-7). Not modelled: Louet 2002 (Fig 5) shows that the cAMP-responsive unit of L-CPT I needs both the CRE (CREB) and a DR1 bound by HNF4alpha, with PGC-1alpha coactivating HNF4alpha (Fig 10C); the PGC-1alpha/HNF4alpha route was removed on 2026-11-06 because a requirement for PGC-1alpha was not tested (overexpression and timing only, rat hepatocytes). PPARalpha: AMPK -> PPARalpha 'directly positively regulates' (Lv 2017 PMID:28515141, full text: AMPK consensus site Ser452; AICAR increases PPARalpha Ser/Thr phosphorylation in mouse hepatocytes, Fig 4B; constitutively active AMPK phosphorylates WT but not S452A PPARalpha in HEK293T/HepG2 cells, Fig 4C-D, and activates WT but not S452A PPARalpha in mouse hepatocytes, Fig 4E; cell-based, no in-vitro kinase assay, pan phospho-Ser/Thr antibody) -> Cpt1a (ISS human PPARA UniProtKB:Q07869, PPRE in CPT1A PMID:16271724; IMP PMID:28515141 Suppl Fig 4A-C, PPARalpha RNAi in mouse hepatocytes). PPARalpha is required for glucagon-stimulated fatty acid oxidation in mouse hepatocytes (IMP PMID:19046568 Fig 6D). Longuet 2008 (PMID:19046568) reports an AMPK -> p38 MAPK -> PPARalpha route in mouse hepatocytes (Fig 2C, 5J-L); not modelled, the direct edge follows Lv 2017. Caveat: Louet 2002 reports that cAMP induction of L-CPT I is not abolished in PPARalpha-/- hepatocytes, so PPARalpha is not required for the cAMP/PKA transcriptional route. PKA cAMP/PKA signal transduction (GO:0141156) part of positive regulation of fatty acid beta-oxidation (added 2026-11-10): IC with GO:0001228 (downstream Creb1 transcription activator activity, part of GO:0032000), reference Louet 2002 (PMID:12107181, Fig 6: PKA-Calpha induces the L-CPT I promoter in rat hepatocytes); no PKA loss-of-function study with a hepatic FAO readout was found. A second, CREB-independent route is reported by Assali 2026 (PMID:42641876, full text): glucagon induces PKA-dependent NCLX Ser258 phosphorylation (human HepG2, H-89 sensitive, Fig 3B-C, 3G-H) and hepatocyte NCLX knockout blunts glucagon-stimulated lipolysis and FAO and raises CPT1 malonyl-CoA sensitivity in mice; H-89 was not tested on FAO; NCLX (Slc8b1) not modelled. Creb1 transcription activator activity part of positive regulation of fatty acid beta-oxidation (GO:0032000): IC with GO:0004095 (Cpt1a carnitine O-palmitoyltransferase activity, which Creb1 indirectly positively regulates), reference Louet 2002 (PMID:12107181, Fig 6: CREB drives the cAMP response of the L-CPT I promoter); Louet measures CPT1 gene expression, not FAO; rat ISS (RGD:620218) on this fact replaced 2026-11-10."^^xsd:string ,
                                                               "cAMP/PKA trunk (updated 2026-11-10): Gcgr has input Gnas; Gcgr -> Gnas -> Adcy6 -> cAMP -> PKA (Prkaca) -> Creb1 (see Transcriptional arms). Gnas has input Adcy6 (added 2026-11-09): ISS from human GNAS (UniProtKB:P63092) based on Bhagirath 2022 (PMID:35664968, human AC6 and Galphas in HEK293T: co-IP Fig 1C-D; AC6 cAMP response to constitutively active Galphas Fig 4D). Adcy6 is the hepatic isoform per Jia 2020 (PMID:31918924, full text: liver-enriched adenylate cyclase in mouse primary hepatocyte RNA-seq, Fig 7B; induced by fasting, Fig 7F, and glucagon, Suppl Fig 2; ADCY6 protein in mouse primary hepatocytes, Fig 7C-D = basis of the hepatocyte IDA). Jia 2020 has no Gs data, so Gnas -> Adcy6 ('has input Adcy6' and 'directly positively regulates' adenylate cyclase activity) is evidenced by ISS from human GNAS (UniProtKB:P63092), whose G protein activity is IDA PMID:17110384 (Gao 2007: purified constitutively active Galphas stimulates human AC6 adenylyl cyclase activity in Sf9 membranes, Fig 1D, 2B, and rat AC6, Fig 3B); Bhagirath 2022 (PMID:35664968) supports the same ISS (2026-11-10). cAMP measurements (PMID:23292513) are not isoform-resolved."^^xsd:string ;
                                                  <https://w3id.org/biolink/vocab/in_taxon> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_10090> .

